The reference walker enumerates construct+type combinations matching a stroke sequence by maintaining an obligation stack: appending an arity-N type pushes N argument slots; later type strokes fill them in D12 order; a sequence is terminal iff all slots are filled and no strokes remain unmatched. It produces a resolved template (literal chunks substituted for type slots) and a terminal flag, or `None` if no construct matches.

`walk(strokes, types, constructs) -> Option<WalkResult>` is the entry point: it finds the highest-specificity construct whose base matches the stroke sequence head, then walks its slots against residual type strokes. Specificity favors longer bases and fused shapes (when `c.shape` is `Some`, the next stroke must subtract to a valid type, adding +1 to the score). `match_base` returns `None` if the base doesn't match, the shape stroke doesn't subtract cleanly, or the residual doesn't exist in the types table; `walk_construct` fills slots in the order specified by `c.fill_order`, failing if a stroke is invalid or extra strokes remain after all slots are filled.

`render_walk(result, profile)` wraps the resolved template in a synthetic `TypedEntry` and delegates to the same render/snippet functions the enumerated `dict.steno` path uses — this guarantees golden vectors are trustworthy by construction. It must stay in sync with the template format that `render_plain`, `render_smart`, and `render_snippet` expect: non-type chunks (`Landing`/`BodyBreak`/`Brace`/`Newline`/`Tab`/`Lit`) are preserved intact, and `one_liner = false` so `%b` always breaks. `consume_type` (in `consume` module) is the type-slot matching engine; `subtract_strokes` and `lookup` are imported from the type system.